A Fe—Cr—Al stainless steel invention suitable for use as a flexible or rigid substrate for thin-film solar cells may be summarized, in formal patent style, as follows:

## Technical Summary

The invention provides a ferritic Fe—Cr—Al stainless steel sheet or foil for a solar cell substrate, the steel having high heat resistance, oxidation resistance, dimensional stability during semiconductor deposition, and an electrically insulating, chemically stable surface oxide film suitable for supporting a thin-film photovoltaic stack.

## Alloy Composition

The substrate material comprises, in mass percent:

- Cr: about 10 to 25%;
- Al: about 1 to 8%;
- C: not more than about 0.05%;
- Si: not more than about 1.0%;
- Mn: not more than about 1.0%;
- P: not more than about 0.05%;
- S: not more than about 0.01%;
- N: not more than about 0.05%;
- optionally one or more of Ti, Nb, Zr, Hf, Y, La, Ce, or other rare-earth elements in a total amount of about 0.001 to 0.5%; and
- the balance Fe and unavoidable impurities.

In preferred embodiments, the Cr content is selected to impart corrosion and oxidation resistance, while the Al content is selected to form a continuous alumina-based insulating film during heat treatment. Optional reactive elements such as Y, Ce, La, Zr, or Hf improve adhesion and thermal stability of the oxide film.

## Surface Oxide Profile

The steel substrate includes, on at least one principal surface thereof, a thermally formed oxide layer comprising an Al-rich oxide. The oxide layer preferably includes:

- a continuous alumina-containing layer, primarily Al₂O₃;
- a thickness of about 10 nm to 2 μm, more preferably about 50 nm to 500 nm;
- an Al concentration in the oxide layer greater than the Al concentration in the base metal;
- reduced Fe and Cr content at the outermost surface relative to the substrate interior;
- high electrical resistivity sufficient to electrically isolate the solar cell back electrode from the metallic substrate; and
- good adhesion to the Fe—Cr—Al stainless steel base material after repeated heating and cooling.

In certain embodiments, the oxide film has a layered structure including an outer Al-rich oxide region and an underlying Cr-containing transition oxide region. The surface oxide suppresses diffusion of Fe, Cr, and other metallic elements into the photovoltaic functional layers and provides a stable barrier during high-temperature solar cell fabrication.

## Production Method

The invention further provides a method of producing the solar cell substrate, comprising:

1. melting and casting an Fe—Cr—Al stainless steel having the above composition;
2. hot rolling the cast material to form a hot-rolled strip;
3. optionally annealing and pickling the hot-rolled strip;
4. cold rolling the strip to a desired sheet or foil thickness;
5. final annealing the cold-rolled material to obtain a ferritic structure and suitable flatness;
6. subjecting at least one surface of the steel to a controlled oxidation treatment in an oxidizing or low-dew-point atmosphere at about 700°C to 1,200°C; and
7. forming, by selective oxidation of Al, a continuous alumina-based surface oxide layer.

The oxidation treatment may be performed in air, oxygen-containing gas, water-vapor-controlled gas, or inert gas containing a controlled oxygen partial pressure. Prior to oxidation, the surface may be polished, degreased, pickled, or otherwise cleaned to promote uniform oxide formation.

## Functional Advantages

The resulting Fe—Cr—Al stainless steel substrate provides:

- high thermal stability during thin-film solar cell processing;
- flexibility and mechanical strength superior to brittle ceramic or glass substrates;
- reduced contamination of photovoltaic absorber layers;
- an integral insulating oxide surface without requiring a separately deposited insulating film;
- improved adhesion between the substrate and subsequently deposited back electrode or semiconductor layers; and
- suitability for thin-film solar cells such as CIGS, CZTS, CdTe, amorphous silicon, or perovskite-based devices.
